.TH udpsend 8  "2026-11-27" "USER COMMANDS"
.SH NAME
udpsend \- Trace UDP sendmsg() calls. Uses Linux eBPF/bcc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B udpsend [\-h] [\-T] [\-t] [\-d] [\-P PORT]
.SH DESCRIPTION
This tool traces UDP sendmsg() calls for both IPv4 and IPv6, showing the
process name, PID, UID, IP version, source and destination IP addresses and
ports.

The probes cover sendmsg() attempts that reach udp_sendmsg() or udpv6_sendmsg(),
including failed calls. Calls rejected earlier in the syscall path (for
example, by an LSM hook or invalid file descriptor) are not traced. Calls
forwarded from udpv6_sendmsg() to udp_sendmsg() are reported as IPv4.

The tool tracks in-flight IPv6 calls in an LRU hash map so orphaned entries
from missed return probes are automatically evicted. Diagnostics are printed
to standard error if a map update fails, if the same thread re-enters
udpv6_sendmsg() with a leftover entry, or if an entry is evicted while the
return probe is reading it (entries evicted before the return probe's lookup
are silently dropped).

This works by tracing the kernel udp_sendmsg() and udpv6_sendmsg() functions
using dynamic tracing, and will need updating to match any changes to these
functions.

Note that for normal userspace sends, the kernel runs inet_autobind() before
entering udp_sendmsg() or udpv6_sendmsg(), so SPORT is already assigned even on
a socket's first send. For unbound, unconnected sockets, SADDR will remain
0.0.0.0 (or ::) on every send because the kernel does not store the
route-selected source address on the socket.

Since this uses BPF, only the root user can use this tool.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Linux >= 4.10 (Linux >= 4.18 for \-d/\-\-dns), CONFIG_BPF, and bcc.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\-h
Print usage message.
.TP
\-T
Include a wall-clock time column (HH:MM:SS) when the event is read by the tool.
.TP
\-t
Include a timestamp column (seconds since trace start).
.TP
\-d, \-\-dns
Inspect UDP payloads (up to 4096 bytes total message length) across all ports
and decode standard DNS queries (first question QNAME and QTYPE) into a
trailing DNS column. Requires Linux >= 4.18. Non-DNS packets, DNS responses,
corked continuations, or payloads whose header/question is split across
multiple iovecs or exceeds the 256-byte capture prefix are displayed as "-".
.TP
\-P PORT, \-\-port PORT
Comma-separated list of destination ports to trace (filtered in-kernel).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Trace all UDP sendmsg() calls:
#
.B udpsend
.TP
Trace all UDP sendmsg() calls, and include seconds since trace start:
#
.B udpsend \-t
.TP
Trace all UDP sendmsg() calls, and include wall-clock time:
#
.B udpsend \-T
.TP
Trace UDP sendmsg() calls to destination port 53 only:
#
.B udpsend \-P 53
.TP
Trace UDP sendmsg() calls to destination ports 53 and 5353 and decode DNS
queries:
#
.B udpsend \-P 53,5353 \-\-dns
.SH FIELDS
.TP
TIME
Wall-clock time (HH:MM:SS) when the event is read by the tool.
.TP
TIME(s)
Time of the call, in seconds since trace start.
.TP
PID
Process ID.
.TP
UID
User ID.
.TP
COMM
Process name.
.TP
IP
IP address family (4 or 6).
.TP
SADDR
Source IP address.
.TP
SPORT
Source port.
.TP
DADDR
Destination IP address.
.TP
DPORT
Destination port.
.TP
DNS
Decoded DNS query in the form "query QNAME QTYPE" when \-d/\-\-dns is enabled,
or "-" if the datagram is not a decodable standard DNS query.
.SH OVERHEAD
This traces the kernel udp_sendmsg() and udpv6_sendmsg() functions and prints
output for each event. As the rate of UDP sends is generally expected to be
low, the overhead is also expected to be negligible. If you have an application
that is calling a high rate of UDP sends, such as a busy DNS server or QUIC
service, test and understand this overhead before use.
.SH SOURCE
This is from bcc.
.IP
https://github.com/iovisor/bcc
.PP
Also look in the bcc distribution for a companion _example.txt file containing
example usage, output, and commentary for this tool.
